      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Colder, nutrient‑rich upwelling zones support higher primary productivity, which fuels anchovy populations.",
      "evidence": "Oceanographic research shows upwelling brings cold water to the surface, increasing phytoplankton biomass; anchovies feed on zooplankton that thrive on this productivity.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Global anchovy biomass is concentrated in temperate upwelling regions (e.g., off California, Peru, Northwest Africa).",
      "evidence": "FAO and NOAA assessments of marine fish stocks list the highest anchovy catches and biomass in these cooler upwelling systems, accounting for >70 % of reported global anchovy catch.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "While some anchovy species inhabit warm tropical waters, their combined abundance is far lower than that of the temperate populations.",
      "evidence": "Regional surveys in the Indo‑Pacific show tropical anchovy densities an order of magnitude lower than those in temperate upwelling zones.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore, more anchovies live in colder temperature waters than in warmer waters.",
      "evidence": "Aggregating the evidence from steps 1‑4 indicates the majority of global anchovy biomass resides in cooler, nutrient‑rich regions.",
